WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Penetration and puncture injuries can be a bit more complex than a standard cut because of what might be happening beneath the surface.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.000
A penetration wound happens when an object breaks through the skin and stays inside the body,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:20.000
while a puncture occurs when a pointed object, like a nail, needle, or piece of glass, pushes in and then comes right back out.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Because these injuries can cause significant internal bleeding that you can't see,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
we always treat them as a serious emergency and call 911 immediately.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
If a foreign object is stuck inside the body, the golden rule is, do not remove the object.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.000
As tempting as it might be to pull it out, that item may actually be acting like a plug to help slow down the bleeding.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
The only time you would ever remove it is if it is directly blocking the person's airway,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
or if you absolutely must move it to perform life-saving CPR.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Otherwise, do your best to stabilize the object and apply firm pressure around it, not directly on it,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
to control any external bleeding while you wait for professional help.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
For much smaller, everyday issues like splinters, the process is a bit different.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Start by washing your hands and the affected area thoroughly.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Once you've located the splinter, use clean tweezers to gently pull the item out,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
making sure to pull in the exact same direction that it entered the skin to avoid breaking it.

